    "created_at": "2017-05-25T20:00:14Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-05-25T20:00:14Z",
    "body": "> Could we handle this by special casing EventTarget.addEventListener() when the callback is a built-in function?\r\n\r\nNo, it's a problem that applies to all callback-accepting APIs in general, right?  Certainly if they can then be induced to call that callback while script is on the stack.\r\n\r\n> Notice that skip-when-determining-incumbent counter is incremented before we invoke sendMessageFromAToB()\r\n\r\nLet's walk through https://heycam.github.io/webidl/#es-invoking-callback-functions carefully here:\r\n\r\n* In step 8, we prepare to run script with the settings object for the global of the sendMessageFromAToB.  This pushes the realm execution context of A on the execution context stack.\r\n* In step 9, we prepare to run a callback with the settings object passed being whatever was the callback context.  We push that on the backup incumbent settings object stack.  Then we look for the topmost script-having execution context.  The realm execution context for A has no ScriptOrModule, so we keep going and find the execution context for the global script running in B; we increment its \"skip\" counter.\r\n\r\nThen later when the postMessage call happens and we land in https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#incumbent-settings-object now the \"topmost script-having execution context\" is the execution context for the invocation of sendMessageFromAToB, which has a zero skip counter, and hence the incumbent is the Realm of the sendMessageFromAToB function, which is A.",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "pin": null,
