    "created_at": "2017-06-02T22:52:00Z",
    "updated_at": "2017-06-02T22:52:00Z",
    "body": "I must confess I haven't dug into every issue here, partially because @bzbarsky seems to be on top of things so far. In particular he seems to have helped reason out why the incumbent is A, not B, in this case. But let me point out some things that don't seem to have been addressed:\r\n\r\n> (*) As far as I can tell the standard does not explicitly mention that the incumbent settings/global object is saved when registering an event listener.\r\n\r\nThis is taken care of in Web IDL, for all callbacks (not just timer ones): https://heycam.github.io/webidl/#es-callback-function\r\n\r\n> In section Event Handler it quotes non-existent text from the DOM spec (last updated 9 May 2017):\r\n\r\nIt's not quoting the DOM spec, it's referencing it. In particular it's effectively saying \"this is all related to EventTarget, which is a DOM concept; please go look that up in [DOM] if you want more details on what an EventTarget is.\"\r\n\r\nThe confusion here is that when you do `.onfoo = bar`, you don't actually add `bar` as an event listener to the EventTarget's list of event listeners. You add a synthetic `EventListener` that runs the event handler processing algorithm, and eventually itself calls the Web IDL-callback-version-of-`bar`. But the Web IDL-callback-version-of-`bar` has already been converted from `bar`, and so it *does* have a callback context, so any spec text that actually cares about callback context will end up looking at that callback context.\r\n\r\nHope this helps?",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
